{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `resumeOrHandleFailedCli` is dead code — the extracted helper is never called**

`resumeOrHandleFailedCli` was extracted alongside `resumeOrHandleInterrupted`, but the action's failed-CLI branch still carries the same logic **inline** (`js/pushReworkChanges.js:2094-2108`):

```javascript
if (isFailedCliReworkResponse(fixSummary)) {
    var failedResume = tryResumeAgent({ ... stage: 'rework_cli_failed', ... });
    if (failedResume.attempted) {
        return action(params);
    }
    return handleFailedReworkCli(tracker, ticketKey, branchName, _customParams, statuses, fixSummary);
}
```

So the helper at line 1884 is never invoked from anywhere (verified: no call sites outside its own definition). Consequences:

- The PR description lists it among the extracted helpers, but only 3 of the 4 resume paths actually use their helper — the duplication the extraction was meant to remove still exists.
- It is untestable dead weight: no test can cover it via the public `action` path, so it sits at 0% coverage in the CRAP ratchet (it passes the `<= 8` threshold only because its CC is low — but it still pollutes the baseline and can silently rot).
- The inline duplicate and the dead helper can drift apart (e.g. the inline path passes `_customParams`, the helper takes `customParams` — a future edit to one will not affect the other).

Either call it from the failed-CLI branch (mirroring `resumeOrHandleInterrupted`):

```javascript
if (isFailedCliReworkResponse(fixSummary)) {
    var failedOutcome = resumeOrHandleFailedCli(ticketKey, _customParams, tracker, branchName, statuses, fixSummary);
    if (failedOutcome.resume) {
        return action(params);
    }
    return failedOutcome.result;
}
```

…or delete the helper. Behaviour is unchanged either way (the helper body is a verbatim lift of the inline block), and the existing `rework_cli_failed` characterization test will keep pinning the branch.
